#.vscode/

# Flutter/Dart/Pub related
**/doc/api/
**/ios/Flutter/.last_build_id
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
.pub-cache/
.pub/
/build/
/coverage/

# Symbolication related
app.*.symbols

# Obfuscation related
app.*.map.json

# Android Studio will place build artifacts here
/android/app/debug
/android/app/profile
/android/app/release

# Signing / credentials — never commit these
key.properties
android/key.properties
# UNANCHORED on purpose. These were `android/app/*.jks` only, which did not
# cover a keystore generated in the repo ROOT — where `keytool -keystore
# afos-release.jks` puts it if you run it from the project directory, which is
# exactly what the documented command in .github/workflows/main.yml does. This
# repo is PUBLIC; leaking the release key lets anyone forge an update, and it
# can never be rotated for already-installed apps.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pem
# UNANCHORED for the same reason as *.jks above. These were anchored to
# `android/app/` and `ios/Runner/`, which is only where they live for THIS
# app. A Firebase config for an unrelated project — copied into the repo
# under its own folder — slipped straight past that: `hostel_house_andoird
# version/android/app/google-services.json` sat in this PUBLIC repo's history
# carrying a live API key and OAuth client ids. Match the filename anywhere.
#
# GLOBBED, not the bare filename. `google-services.json` matched only the exact
# name, and a browser download of a second copy is called
# `google-services (2).json` — which sailed past it and sat untracked-but-
# committable in a PUBLIC repo, carrying a live API key and the project id.
# That is the same leak described above arriving by a different route, so the
# pattern now covers every variant a download or an editor backup can produce.
google-services*.json
GoogleService-Info*.plist
.env
.env.*

# Ad-hoc SQL scripts dropped in the repo ROOT. Real migrations live in
# supabase/migrations/ and real tooling in tool/, both of which stay tracked —
# this pattern is root-only (`/`) and does not touch either.
#
# The file this exists to prevent was `manual_admin_assignment.sql`: a
# throwaway script that spelled out, in a public repo, the exact statement for
# granting yourself super_admin. It carried no password, but it was a working
# recipe published next to the database it targeted — and it turned out the
# escalation it described was genuinely possible (fixed in migration
# 20260814222540). Scratch SQL written against production does not belong in
# version control.
/*.sql

# Supabase CLI local state (per-machine, not project config)
supabase/.temp/

# Playwright MCP tool output
.playwright-mcp/
.aidesigner/*
!.aidesigner/.gitkeep

# --- Agent / editor scratch directories -------------------------------------
# THIS REPO IS PUBLIC. Anything committed here is world-readable forever, so
# tool configs and assistant working directories do not belong in it: they are
# machine-local, they leak paths and workflow details, and none of them is
# needed to build or run AFOS.
#
# .codex/ was tracked until 2026-08-16 and has been removed.
.codex/
.claude/
.cursor/
# Same family, caught 2026-08-16 when a `git add -A` swept 64 of their files
# into a staged commit. Never pushed, so they have no history here -- but the
# near miss is the argument for listing every one of them rather than the two
# that happened to exist when this block was written.
#
# .agents/skills/ in particular is a vendored copy of assistant skill packs:
# thousands of lines of tooling documentation that say a great deal about how
# this repo is worked on and nothing about how to build or run AFOS.
.agents/
.codebuddy/
.junie/
.kiro/
.playwright-mcp/
skills-lock.json
# MCP server declarations are machine-local (paths, which servers are wired
# up). This one carries no credentials -- checked -- but it still describes a
# private workflow, and the next edit to it might not be as harmless.
.mcp.json

# --- Internal working documents ---------------------------------------------
# Removed from the repo 2026-08-16. All of these still exist on the maintainer's
# machine; they are simply not the public's business, and they were the answer
# to "why can anyone see how we work on this".
#
# CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md    the project constitution. Two copies of the same
#                          document, and line 113 of CLAUDE.md published a
#                          machine path: C:\RakibFlutter\bin\flutter.bat.
# audit/                   internal assessments -- SLOP_REPORT ("baseline
#                          violation count"), PARITY_REPORT, BUG_REGISTER,
#                          CONTRACT_MAP. A running list of this project's own
#                          weaknesses, published next to the code.
# .gitlab-ci.yml           a stale pipeline for a CI system this project does
#                          not use (GitHub Actions does the work). It named
#                          SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY as an expected variable --
#                          no value, but it told a reader exactly which secret
#                          to go looking for.
#
# What stays public, deliberately: README.md is written FOR outsiders, and
# supabase/migrations/ is the schema's source of truth -- hiding it would be
# hiding the security model reviewers are invited to check.
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
audit/
.gitlab-ci.yml
.aider*
.continue/
.windsurfrules
.gemini/
.opencode/
.roo/
CLAUDE.local.md

# docs/superpowers/  implementation plans and specs -- same category as audit/
#                    above: they describe how the work is done and enumerate
#                    this project's unfinished edges. Kept on the maintainer's
#                    machine, not published. Added 2026-08-22 after a plan was
#                    pushed here by accident.
docs/superpowers/

# REDESIGN_LOG.md    the engineering diary, 311 KB of it. Same category as
#                    audit/ and missed when that went in 2026-08-16: it is a
#                    phase-by-phase account of every defect found in this
#                    project, written in the voice of someone talking to
#                    themselves, and it names a live test account. Still
#                    REQUIRED reading and writing for the maintainer -- the
#                    workflow rule "append every change to REDESIGN_LOG.md"
#                    stands. It just is not the public's business.
# docs/MAIL_SETUP.md the mail runbook. Names every secret the mail path needs,
#                    the provider's daily allowance, and each rate-limit
#                    bucket's capacity and refill. No VALUES -- checked -- but
#                    together they are a map of exactly how to exhaust the
#                    signup mail budget, and the triage table is written for
#                    whoever is on call, not for a reviewer.
#
# Untracked 2026-09-03, both kept on disk. This is the answer to "hide the docs
# that could harm" that does NOT require the repository to be private: the code,
# the 230 migrations and the 60 tests stay public. Authorization lives in
# Postgres, not in secrecy --
# verified anonymously against the live API: profiles returns [],
# pending_registrations and email_outbox return 401, app_secrets 404.
REDESIGN_LOG.md
docs/MAIL_SETUP.md

# --- Untracked 2026-09-03, second pass -------------------------------------
# All three still exist on disk. Removed from the repository, not from the
# machine, for the same reason as everything above: the repo is going back to
# public and these are either operational or an enumeration aid.
#
# SECURITY.md        the disclosure policy. Read on its own it is fine -- it is
#                    the inventory that is not: it names the exact tables and
#                    mechanisms holding the line (user_permissions,
#                    permission_audit, VR-ID token issuing, the APK signing
#                    path) and states which client-side checks are decorative.
#                    Private vulnerability reporting still works with no file
#                    present; GitHub offers it from the Security tab.
# RELEASING.md       the release runbook, and operational rather than
#                    explanatory: the signing steps, the CI secrets the tag job
#                    consumes, the app_releases row that makes every installed
#                    phone offer an update, and a machine path
#                    (C:\RakibFlutter\bin\flutter.bat). A reader who wants to
#                    know how a build is produced is not the reader it is for.
# integration_test/  overflow_smoke_test.dart runs against PRODUCTION Supabase
#                    with five persistent QA accounts and names every one of
#                    them, including a super_admin. No passwords -- they come
#                    from --dart-define -- but publishing the list hands a
#                    reader the five addresses worth guessing at, and the file
#                    also carries a maintainer machine path. The dev_dependency
#                    in pubspec.yaml stays, so the suite still runs here.
# docs/              ignored wholesale now rather than file by file. Everything
#                    that has ever been in it (superpowers/ plans, MAIL_SETUP)
#                    was internal, and the next thing written there will be too.
SECURITY.md
RELEASING.md
integration_test/
docs/
